-- demand.lth: the demand as a value: what an action asks of a program, with every cutoff it
-- carries; what it passes down to each law of the cone; what a run records of it.
--
-- Draft 0, 2026-10-15, by Claude Fable 5.1, an agent of the coordinating session, on the brief
-- record/IA/demand-2026-10-15/prompt.md (step 6.1 of
-- record/plan/luthier-from-zero/plan-from-zero-2026-10-13-execution.md). Sources: spec 8.S1, 8.S2,
-- 8.S3, 8.S8 (docs/spec/08-demand.md); 6.S10, 6.S14, 6.S15; 12.S13, 12.S17, 12.S18; L5.17, L5.18,
-- L5.19, L12.3, P11; D-2026-10-13-f (the demand is open), D-2026-10-13-m (the cost types come
-- later), D-2026-10-14-c (first everything Boolean, the cutoffs as parameters the demand
-- supplies); certification.lth §3 (the demand on one law, `serves`, `Certified`), test.lth §3
-- (`Grade`), generators.lth §6 (`Budgets`, the same rule: every budget an argument). Pattern of
-- certification.lth.
-- 2026-10-15, by Claude Opus 5.5, an agent of the coordinating session, on record/IA/
-- layer-3-review-2026-10-15/prompt-fix-BCD.md and the coordinator's addition to it (B19 of its
-- summary-BCD.md, Fable's first change for this file): `Demanding` had no model that could fail
-- a law (two laws fixed its two operations outright, two were laws of `Certifying`), so
-- `lawDemand` and `recorded` are definitions, the two laws of `Certifying` at the demand passed
-- down stay as predicates for certification.lth to claim, and the one law a model can break, that
-- one identity has one result, is the law of a theory whose value is the store, over the identity
-- as a parameter (which identity is item 5 of the summary, Luciano's question).
-- 2026-10-16, by Claude Opus 5.5, an agent of the coordinating session, on record/IA/
-- code-follows-2026-10-16/prompt-store.md (D-2026-10-15-h, item 5 answered): the record carries the
-- decoration, so that the key of a run is a function of its record, its seed, its plan and the
-- certificates (plan.lth's `keyOf`); `determined` speaks of the key (§4).
-- 2026-10-16, by Claude Opus 5.5, an agent of the coordinating session, on record/IA/
-- code-follows-2026-10-16/prompt-modes.md: the modes of running are fields of the demand
-- (D-2026-10-16-d): the strategy (moved here from plan.lth, which imports it), the fuel optional,
-- a time limit, `fresh`; and the provisional externs the demand runs as assumed, by name
-- (D-2026-10-16-c).
--
-- What this file states. 8.S1 says a demand is open: whatever the program declares as a
-- parameter, the demand may set; its form is a library's (D-2026-10-12-al), so what is here is
-- one library's record, with the operations of 8.S2 that the core asks for. D-2026-10-14-c says
-- first everything Boolean, so the cutoff is the weakest grade accepted, and where a cost would
-- enter (the bound, the evidence cost, the price of an assumption, 6.S15, 6.S10) there is a hole
-- `C`, with the comment "D-2026-10-13-m, after the slice's failures". certification.lth §3 keeps
-- its `Demand`, the demand on one law; here it is `LawDemand`, the same record by structure, and
-- the law of 8.S1 is that it is passed down from the run's demand and not recomputed (L12.3).
-- The demand of a run is built from the arguments `run` takes (demand_model.lut, `theDemand`),
-- with its defaults declared there and recorded (L5.19); what it passes down and what it records
-- are definitions of it. What the brief or the spec leaves open is marked [proposed] at the item
-- above it.
--
-- Index (all sections written in this draft):
--   §1 The demand: its fields, the holes for the costs and the decorated type (8.S1, 8.S2,
--      6.S15, 6.S10, 12.S18, 12.S13).                                                       done
--   §2 Passing down: the demand on one law, the demand an inner action receives (L12.3,
--      6.S15, 6.S10).                                                                       done
--   §3 What a run records of its demand (12.S18, L5.18).                                     done
--   §4 The theory `Store`: one identity, one result (8.S3, 8.S9).                           done
--   §5 The laws of `Certifying` at the demand passed down (6.S10, 6.S16).                   done
--   §6 The strategy that ran: a specimen of the one asked (8.S4, D-2026-10-16-d).           done

import "certification.lth" Certifying Entry EntryFor Value Hash C ServesIsMonotoneInTheCutoff
import "test.lth" Grade Sampled AssumedGrade gradeRank
import "term.lth" Term

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- §1 The demand

-- The cost type of 6.S4, and what a demand carries in it (6.S15: the cutoff is a bound and an
-- evidence cost in `C`; 6.S10: the evidence cost it assigns to assumptions, with no default):
-- holes. D-2026-10-13-m, after the slice's failures. In this draft the cutoff is the weakest
-- grade accepted (`accepts`, D-2026-10-14-c) and the price of an assumption is whether that grade
-- admits `AssumedGrade` (§2). `C` is certification.lth's hole, the same cost type (core bite 13b).
bound : Demand -> C
evidence : Demand -> C
assumed : Demand -> Maybe C

-- The decorated type (8.S1): asking for `t : P T` where the program computes a `T` asks the cone
-- of `t` to build a `P T`, the cone written generic over a type operator `G`, the identity by
-- default, which the demand replaces by `P`. [proposed, 8.S1] Whether the generic form is written
-- by hand or derived from the cone is open, and so is the interface asked of `P` beyond the
-- functor and applicative laws of 8.S2's table; both are holes. A demand that names no
-- decoration asks for the cone's own type, the identity.
Decoration : U
decorate : Decoration -> Term -> Term

-- The strategy, a mode of running and not of the program (8.S4, L5.2, P11, D-2026-10-16-d): forward
-- on demand, the default 8.S3 decides (the live subgraph in topological order, computed
-- strictly); or call by need (each name reduced at its first demand and kept), which computes the
-- same live subgraph in the order of first demand. What each constructor means is an order, stated
-- as a law of plan.lth's `Planning` (its §5, L2.7); `TheDefaultsAgree` holds the two to the same
-- result, which is what lets run.py's call by need be the specimen that computes a demand for
-- forward on demand (8.S4).
Strategy : U := data {
  ForwardOnDemand : Strategy ;
  CallByNeed : Strategy ;
}

-- A demand (8.S1): the entry; the type demanded, the identity when `nothing`; the cutoff, the
-- weakest grade accepted (6.S15, D-2026-10-14-c; `run --accept`); the provisional externs it runs
-- as assumed, by name (`run --accept assumed:NAME`, D-2026-10-16-c: a provisional is refused unless
-- named, whatever the cutoff, so a forgotten one still refuses the run); the confidence for
-- sampled evidence (D-2026-10-12-r); the values of the program's holes (`run --set`, 12.S18);
-- whether every value an extern gives is tested against its refinements as it crosses (`run
-- --checked`, 12.S18, P11); the lock, the interpreter's revision the program's directory pins
-- (12.S13), `nothing` when none does; and the modes of running (D-2026-10-16-d): the strategy
-- (`run --strategy`), the limits on resources, none by default (`run --fuel`, in reduction steps;
-- `run --time-limit`, in seconds; fuel only cuts a run, D-2026-10-15-g), and whether the run
-- executes whatever the store of results holds (`run --fresh`, L5.20).
-- [proposed] The entry is a name; 8.S1's term as entry waits for an action that asks for one.
-- [proposed] `delta` is `nothing` in this draft, since no statistic becomes a cost before
-- D-2026-10-13-m; when one does it is required as `assumed` is, with no default. Every field is a
-- parameter the first demand takes as a declared default argument and records (L5.18, L5.19);
-- none is a constant of the core.
Demand = { entry : String, decoration : Maybe Decoration, accepts : Grade, assumes : List String,
           delta : Maybe Float64, fuel : Maybe Nat, timeLimit : Maybe Float64, sets : List (String * Value),
           checked : Bool, pinned : Maybe String, strategy : Strategy, fresh : Bool }

-- the value a demand gives a hole of the program, if it gives one (12.S18: a hole left without a
-- value is an error that asks for `--set`)
given (d : Demand) (name : String) : Maybe Value := lookup List String Value name d.sets

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- §2 Passing down

-- The demand on one law (certification.lth §3, its `Demand`, 6.S15, 7.S15): the law, the current
-- hash of its cone, what the run gives its parameters, and the weakest grade accepted. The same
-- record by structure, so a `LawDemand` is what `Certifying.serves` and `refuse` take.
LawDemand = { law : String, cone : Hash, given : List (String * Value), accepts : Grade }

-- Whether a demand admits assumptions (6.S10) in the Boolean draft: its cutoff admits
-- `AssumedGrade`, the lowest grade (test.lth §3). The price of an assumption in `C` is the hole
-- `assumed` above; `nothing` there is what refuses every assumed value when the cost types come.
-- [proposed] Until then `run --accept assumed` is where a demand names that price, for the evidence
-- of the store; a provisional extern is priced only by name (`assumes`, D-2026-10-16-c).
admitsAssumptions (d : Demand) : Bool := gradeRank d.accepts <= gradeRank AssumedGrade

-- The demand on a law of the cone (L12.3, 6.S15): the run's cutoff and the run's values, the
-- law's own name and cone; nothing of it is recomputed from the law's cone. [proposed] The run
-- also gives the law the name it certifies, printed, to the law's model parameter
-- (certification.lth §3, run.py's `certification`); that is the run's binding and not the
-- demand's, so `given` here is what `--set` gave.
lawDemand (d : Demand) (law : String) (cone : Hash) : LawDemand :=
  { law := law, cone := cone, given := d.sets, accepts := d.accepts }

-- The demand an inner action receives (L12.3, L5.17): the caller's demand with the inner entry,
-- every cutoff and mode kept, since a cutoff does not compose and so is passed down, never
-- recomputed from the inner cone.
inner (d : Demand) (entry : String) : Demand :=
  { entry := entry, decoration := d.decoration, accepts := d.accepts, assumes := d.assumes, delta := d.delta,
    fuel := d.fuel, timeLimit := d.timeLimit, sets := d.sets, checked := d.checked, pinned := d.pinned,
    strategy := d.strategy, fresh := d.fresh }

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- §3 What a run records

-- What a run records of its demand (12.S18, `x.run.json`; L5.18: every threshold or level a
-- certificate needs comes from the demand and is recorded with it): the entry, the hash of the
-- cone it ran, every parameter it set, the grade it accepted and the provisionals it runs as
-- assumed, the confidence, the fuel and the time limit given, whether it ran checked, the lock,
-- the type demanded, the strategy asked and whether it was fresh (D-2026-10-16-d: every mode
-- recorded). The time, the output, the fuel spent, the strategy that ran (the plan's) and the
-- states of the cone are the run's, not the demand's, and are not here. [proposed,
-- D-2026-10-15-h] The decoration is recorded (B1 of summary-BCD.md): the key of a result reads it
-- (8.S8, `KeyReadsTheType`), and the key is a function of the record (plan.lth's `keyOf`).
RunRecord = { entry : String, cone : Hash, parameters : List (String * Value), accept : Grade, assumes : List String,
           delta : Maybe Float64, fuel : Maybe Nat, timeLimit : Maybe Float64, checked : Bool, lock : Maybe String,
           decoration : Maybe Decoration, strategy : Strategy, fresh : Bool }
recorded (d : Demand) (h : Hash) : RunRecord :=
  { entry := d.entry, cone := h, parameters := d.sets, accept := d.accepts, assumes := d.assumes, delta := d.delta,
    fuel := d.fuel, timeLimit := d.timeLimit, checked := d.checked, lock := d.pinned, decoration := d.decoration,
    strategy := d.strategy, fresh := d.fresh }

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- §4 The store

-- A store of runs: each result kept under the identity of what produced it. The same demand on
-- the same cone gives the same result (8.S3: results are memoized under the hash of code, inputs
-- and mode; 8.S5: a definition has no effects; 8.S9: a hit serves only an equal key), so a store
-- holds one result per identity. The identity is a parameter `K` with its equality, and
-- [D-2026-10-15-h] the identity of a result is its key: plan.lth's `MemoKey`, by its hash, the
-- `key hash` of each entry of `x.results.json` (plan.lth's `ResultsDetermined`); the record of §3,
-- which run.py's `breaches` still compares in `x.run.json`, is not an identity, since two
-- legitimate runs may differ only in what the record does not carry (a seed under which a sampled
-- certificate passes and one under which it fails, B2).
Stored (K : U) = { key : K, result : String }
agreesWith (K : U) (eq : K -> K -> Bool) (r : Stored K) (rs : List (Stored K)) : Bool :=
  all List (Stored K) (fn (q : Stored K) { not (eq r.key q.key) or r.result == q.result }) rs
determined (K : U) (eq : K -> K -> Bool) (rs : List (Stored K)) : Bool :=
  match rs { nil -> true ; cons r rest -> agreesWith K eq r rest and determined K eq rest }

-- 8.S3, 8.S9: two runs with one identity have one result. The value of the theory is the store;
-- run.py reads `x.results.json` back (8.S10), and no model file gives one.
RunStore (K : U) (eq : K -> K -> Bool) : { runs : List (Stored K) }
OneIdentityOneResult (K : U) (eq : K -> K -> Bool) (st : RunStore K eq) : Bool := determined K eq st.runs
RunStore (K : U) (eq : K -> K -> Bool) := OneIdentityOneResult K eq

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- §5 Certifying at the demand passed down

-- Two laws of certification.lth's `Certifying` at the demand §2 passes down; no demand model can
-- break them, so they are predicates here, for `Certifying` to claim (certification.lth is
-- another session's while this is written). [proposed] Where they go is certification.lth's.
-- 6.S10: a demand that names no price for assumptions refuses every assumed value: no entry with
-- the grade `AssumedGrade` serves a law under it
NoPriceRefusesAssumed (k : Certifying) (d : Demand) (law : String) (cone : Hash) (e : Entry) : Bool :=
  admitsAssumptions d or not (e.grade == just AssumedGrade) or not (k.serves (lawDemand d law cone) e)
-- 6.S16, L10.5: lowering the cutoff loses nothing accepted; certification.lth's law, applied to
-- the demand passed down, not restated, over an entry of that demand's law at its hash, the
-- domain the law now takes (C2 of summary.md, certification.lth draft 1; a drawn `Entry` met it
-- almost never)
LoweringLosesNothing (k : Certifying) (d : Demand) (law : String) (cone : Hash) (e : EntryFor (lawDemand d law cone)) (g : Grade) : Bool :=
  ServesIsMonotoneInTheCutoff k (lawDemand d law cone) e g
-- [core bite 13b] the two laws, gathered so that this file claims them (4.S14)
DemandPassesDown := NoPriceRefusesAssumed & LoweringLosesNothing

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- §6 The strategy that ran

-- A run records the strategy its demand asked (§3) and the strategy that computed it (its plan's,
-- plan.lth's `Plan`). They may differ only where plan.lth's `TheDefaultsAgree` makes one the
-- specimen of the other: a demand for forward on demand may be computed by call by need, which
-- run.py does today (8.S4, D-2026-10-16-d); a demand for call by need is computed by call by need,
-- since nothing holds forward on demand to the order of first demand. The value of the theory is
-- what a run records of the two, its model run.py's `x.run.json` (`demand.strategy`, `plan.strategy`).
-- [proposed] A theory and not a check in run.py, so that "say in the record which ran" is a law the
-- record is held to.
Ran : { asked : Strategy, ran : Strategy }
RanIsASpecimenOfTheAsked (r : Ran) : Bool :=
  match r.asked { ForwardOnDemand -> r.ran == ForwardOnDemand or r.ran == CallByNeed ; CallByNeed -> r.ran == CallByNeed }
Ran := RanIsASpecimenOfTheAsked
